# L9 Cursor Invariants

Treat repository code, configuration, ADRs, and test contracts as the source of truth. Before proposing a material change, consult the most relevant source files; use `reports/repo-index/` before broad codebase searches.

Do not claim a feature is fixed, production-ready, wired, deployed, or tested without recording the exact verification performed and its result. State unknowns explicitly.

Respect L9 governance boundaries. Tool actions, permissions, and high-risk changes must flow through existing governance and approval mechanisms. Do not bypass policy evaluation, audit logging, memory access controls, or protected-file controls.

Keep changes narrow and reversible. Do not rename, move, delete, or bulk-rewrite files unless the task requires it and the impact has been inspected. Never introduce secrets, tokens, passwords, private keys, production host credentials, or unredacted local paths into tracked files.

Use the current L9-native architecture. Do not restore any runtime pattern prohibited by `@archive/suite5/manifest.yaml`, including standalone governance services or duplicate telemetry stores.

When a work item touches a contract, API, policy, schema, or runtime wiring, update focused tests and run the narrowest relevant verification before completion.

Reference sources when needed:
- `@readme/L9-KERNEL-REFERENCE.md`
- `@agents/cursor/docs/CURSOR-L9-INTEGRATION.md`
- `@ci/check_adr_compliance.py`
- `@core/governance/engine.py`
